 "summary": "Creates a disabled person retrofit tax credit.",
 "title": "Creates a disabled person retrofit tax credit",
 "vetoMessages": {
  "items": [
   {
    "billId": {
     "basePrintNo": "A5333",
     "basePrintNoStr": "A5333-2017",
     "printNo": "A5333",
     "session": 2017,
     "version": ""
    },
    "billPage": 0,
    "chapter": 0,
    "lineEnd": 0,
    "lineStart": 0,
    "memoText": "\n \n                         VETO MESSAGE - No. 300\n \nTO THE ASSEMBLY:\n \nI am returning herewith, without my approval, the following bill:\n \nAssembly Bill Number 5333-A, entitled:\n \n    \"AN  ACT  to  amend  the tax law, in relation to creating a disabled\n      person retrofit tax credit\"\n \n    NOT APPROVED\n \n  This bill would establish a new non-refundable tax credit equal to 30%\nof the cost of improvements made by  a  taxpayer  to  install  qualified\nimprovements at the taxpayer's primary residence.\n \n  The  bill's stated goal to incentivize owners of residential buildings\nto retrofit homes for access to the disabled is laudable, but  the  bill\nfaces insurmountable technical and fiscal problems that make it impossi-\nble  to  sign.  As written, the credit is based upon credit claiming and\nallocation provisions that are incomplete, ambiguous, and contradictory.\nWhile the bill would require the Department of Taxation and  Finance  to\nallocate  one  million dollars in credits annually based on the \"date of\nfiling\", the bill does not require any specific application or other tax\ncredit-related filing that would trigger a credit allocation. For  exam-\nple,  there  is  an  implicit requirement that taxpayers file income tax\nreturns that reflect tax  credit  claims  for  improvements  during  the\nprevious year. However, this bill requires the credit to be allocated in\nthe  year in which the improvement was made, leaving unclear the type of\nfiling that is needed to necessitate Tax's  credit  allocation.    Addi-\ntionally,  the bill would require credit claims that exceed the one-mil-\nlion-dollar cap to be rolled to January 1st of  the  next  year.    This\nrollover  runs afoul of the bill's provision that requires the credit to\nbe claimed in the tax year in which the qualified improvement was made.\n \n  In addition, the bill does not specify credit allocation rules,  which\nis in stark contrast to other allocated credits that have detailed stat-\nutory  application  and allocation provisions. Allocated credit programs\nalso generally vest substantive administrative responsibility  in  agen-\ncies  that have the expertise to determine whether the expenses underly-\ning the credit claims are allowable and appropriate. Here, Tax does  not\nhave  the  expertise  to determine, for example, whether the location of\nnew light switches in a specific taxpayer's home would be accessible  to\n\"disabled persons,\" a term that is not defined in the bill.\n \n  The  bill has other technical deficiencies. For example, the bill does\nnot define primary residence  and  does  require  the  residence  to  be\nlocated  in New York State. Additionally, although the bill would estab-\nlish a five-thousand-dollar cap  per  dwelling  per  taxpayer,  multiple\nowners  of the same residence could each be allowed to claim the credit,\neven if those owners did not actually  incur  any  of  the  expenses  to\ninstall the qualified improvement.\n \n  The  fiscal  impact associated with this bill is also problematic. Any\nnew program with a fiscal impact of this magnitude needs to be addressed\nin the context of State budget negotiations.\n \n  While I am constrained to veto this bill, I look  forward  to  working\nwith  the Legislature to develop a program that meets this bill's stated\npurpose of incentivizing owners of  residential  buildings  to  retrofit\nhomes to provide access to the disabled.\n \n  The bill is disapproved.                    (signed) ANDREW M. CUOMO\n \n                               __________\n",
    "signedDate": null,
    "signer": "ANDREW M. CUOMO",
